<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  14.3 Partial Derivatives
-->
<section xml:id="chapter-partial-derivatives" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Partial Derivatives</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
State the definition of the partial derivatives
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m> and
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m> as limits, and interpret each as
the slope of a tangent line to a curve cut from the surface by a vertical
plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute partial derivatives by treating all other variables as
constants, and use the standard notations <m>f_x</m>,
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>, and
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0,y_0)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Compute second partial derivatives, including the mixed partials
<m>f_{xy}</m> and <m>f_{yx}</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Apply the mixed derivative theorem to choose the more convenient order of
differentiation.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
A function of one variable has one derivative, which measures the rate at
which the output changes as the single input changes. A function of two
variables has two inputs, and so it has two rates of change: one obtained
by moving in the <m>x</m>-direction and one by moving in the
<m>y</m>-direction. These are the partial derivatives, and each is an
ordinary one-variable derivative in disguise, taken while the other
variable is held fixed.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-pd-definition">
<title>Definition of Partial Derivatives</title>

<p>
Consider the point <m>P(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> on the surface
<m>z = f(x,y)</m>.
</p>

<p>
The slope of the tangent line in the plane <m>y = y_0</m> at the point
<m>P(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> to the curve <m>z = f(x,y_0)</m> can be
computed by keeping the <m>y</m> fixed at the value <m>y_0</m>, changing
<m>x_0</m> to <m>x_0 + h</m>, and taking the limit of <m>h \to 0</m>.
This is known as the <term>partial derivative of <m>f(x,y)</m> with
respect to <m>x</m></term> and is defined as
<md xml:id="eq-pd-def-x" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{f(x_0+h,\, y_0) - f(x_0,y_0)}{h}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
The slope of the tangent line in the plane <m>x = x_0</m> at the point
<m>P(x_0, y_0, f(x_0,y_0))</m> to the curve <m>z = f(x_0,y)</m> can be
computed by keeping the <m>x</m> fixed at the value <m>x_0</m>, changing
<m>y_0</m> to <m>y_0 + h</m>, and taking the limit of <m>h \to 0</m>.
This is known as the <term>partial derivative of <m>f(x,y)</m> with
respect to <m>y</m></term> and is defined as
<md xml:id="eq-pd-def-y" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \lim_{h\to 0} \frac{f(x_0,\, y_0+h) - f(x_0,y_0)}{h}.
</md>
<xref ref="fig-pd-two-tangents"/> shows both tangent lines at once.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pd-two-tangents">
<caption>
The two curves through <m>P</m> cut from the surface by the vertical
planes <m>y = y_0</m> and <m>x = x_0</m>, and their tangent lines at
<m>P</m>. The slope of the blue line is
<m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m> and the slope of the dark red line
is <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="86%">
<description>
<p>
A grey surface with two curves drawn on it through a magenta point P. One
curve, in green, is the trace in the plane y equals y nought and has a
blue tangent line at P. The other, in amber, is the trace in the plane x
equals x nought and has a dark red tangent line at P.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=12cm, height=9cm,
  view={-35}{20},
  xmin=-2.3, xmax=2.3, ymin=-2.3, ymax=2.3, zmin=0, zmax=4.4,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
]
% the surface
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, draw=gray!55, fill=gray!30,
          fill opacity=0.35, draw opacity=0.4,
          domain=-2:2, y domain=-2:2, samples=15]
  {3 - 0.25*x^2 - 0.45*y^2};
% trace in the plane y = y0 (green)
\addplot3[very thick, green!50!black, domain=-2:2, samples=33]
  ({x},{0.5},{2.8875 - 0.25*x^2});
% trace in the plane x = x0 (amber)
\addplot3[very thick, orange, domain=-2:2, samples=33]
  ({0.5},{x},{2.9375 - 0.45*x^2});
% tangent line in y = y0, slope df/dx = -0.25
\addplot3[very thick, cyan!60!black, domain=-1.6:1.6, samples=2]
  ({0.5+x},{0.5},{2.825 - 0.25*x});
% tangent line in x = x0, slope df/dy = -0.45
\addplot3[very thick, purple!60!black, domain=-1.6:1.6, samples=2]
  ({0.5},{0.5+x},{2.825 - 0.45*x});
% the point P
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.9, color=magenta]
  coordinates {(0.5,0.5,2.825)};
\node[magenta, anchor=west] at (axis cs:0.62,0.55,2.72)
  {$P(x_0,y_0,f(x_0,y_0))$};
% labels with leaders
\node[cyan!60!black, align=center, anchor=south]
  at (axis description cs:0.30,1.0)
  {Tangent line at $P$\\ in the $y=y_0$ plane};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.30,1.0)
  -- (axis cs:-0.9,0.5,3.175);
\node[purple!60!black, align=center, anchor=west]
  at (axis description cs:1.0,0.62)
  {Tangent line at $P$\\ in the $x=x_0$ plane};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:1.0,0.62)
  -- (axis cs:0.5,1.7,2.285);
\node[green!50!black, anchor=east] at (axis description cs:0.0,0.80)
  {$z=f(x,y_0)$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.0,0.80)
  -- (axis cs:-1.55,0.5,2.287);
\node[orange, anchor=east] at (axis description cs:0.02,0.36)
  {$z=f(x_0,y)$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.02,0.36)
  -- (axis cs:0.5,-1.5,1.925);
\node[gray!30!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.0,0.20)
  {$z=f(x,y)$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:1.0,0.20)
  -- (axis cs:1.75,1.6,1.08);
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
To calculate the partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect to <m>x</m>,
we treat <m>y</m> as a constant and differentiate normally with respect
to <m>x</m>, i.e.
<md xml:id="eq-pd-as-ordinary-x" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \left.\frac{d}{dx} f(x,y_0)\right|_{x=x_0}.
</md>
Similarly, to calculate the partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect
to <m>y</m>, we treat <m>x</m> as a constant and differentiate normally
with respect to <m>y</m>, i.e.
<md xml:id="eq-pd-as-ordinary-y" number="yes">
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
= \left.\frac{d}{dy} f(x_0,y)\right|_{y=y_0}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Here are some common notations for the partial derivative with respect to
<m>x</m>:
<md>
\left.\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}
\qquad
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0,y_0)
\qquad
f_x(x_0,y_0).
</md>
Note that similar notation can be used for the partial derivative with
respect to any variable such as <m>y</m> and <m>z</m>.
</p>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-pd-examples">
<title>Examples</title>

<example xml:id="example-pd-paraboloid">
<title>Partial Derivatives of <m>1-x^2-y^2</m></title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> and evaluate the
following partial derivatives at the point
<m>(x_0,y_0) = (1/2, 1/2)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_x</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_y</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert> Treating <m>y</m> as a constant, we have
<m>f_x = -2x</m> and therefore
<m>f_x(1/2,1/2) = -2(1/2) = -1</m>.
</p>
<p>
<alert>B.</alert> Treating <m>x</m> as a constant, we have
<m>f_y = -2y</m> and therefore
<m>f_y(1/2,1/2) = -2(1/2) = -1</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-pd-quotient">
<title>A Quotient with a Trigonometric Term</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{2y}{y + \cos x}</m> and evaluate
the following partial derivatives at the point
<m>(x_0,y_0) = (0,0)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_x</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_y</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert> Treating <m>y</m> as a constant, we have
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
= (2y)\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{1}{y+\cos x}\right)
= (2y)\left(\frac{\sin x}{(y+\cos x)^2}\right)
= \frac{2y \sin x}{(y+\cos x)^2},
</md>
and therefore <m>f_x(0,0) = \dfrac{0}{1} = 0</m>.
</p>
<p>
<alert>B.</alert> Treating <m>x</m> as a constant, we have
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{2y}{y+\cos x}\right)
= \frac{2(y+\cos x) - 2y}{(y+\cos x)^2}
= \frac{2\cos x}{(y+\cos x)^2},
</md>
and therefore <m>f_y(0,0) = \dfrac{2}{1} = 2</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-pd-elliptic">
<title>Partial Derivatives as Slopes on a Surface</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the elliptic paraboloid <m>z = f(x,y) = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m>.
Evaluate <m>f_x(1,1)</m> and <m>f_y(1,1)</m> and explain what these
numbers represent.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
We compute <m>f_x(x,y) = -2x</m>, so
<md>
f_x(1,1) = -2.
</md>
Note that the intersection of the surface
<m>z = f(x,y) = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m> and the plane <m>y = 1</m> is the
parabola <m>z = 2 - x^2</m>, and <m>f_x(1,1) = -2</m> is the slope of the
tangent line to the curve <m>z = 2 - x^2</m> located in the <m>y = 1</m>
plane. This is the curve <m>C_1</m> in
<xref ref="fig-pd-elliptic-x"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pd-elliptic-x">
<caption>
The trace <m>C_1</m> cut from <m>z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m> by the plane
<m>y = 1</m> is the parabola <m>z = 2 - x^2</m>. Its tangent line at
<m>(1,1,1)</m> has slope <m>f_x(1,1) = -2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
A tall dome shaped surface cut by the vertical plane y equals one. The
curve of intersection is drawn in dark blue and labelled C one, and a
magenta tangent line touches it at the point 1, 1, 1.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=10cm, height=9cm,
  view={-40}{16},
  xmin=-2.1, xmax=2.1, ymin=-1.6, ymax=1.6, zmin=0, zmax=4.4,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
]
% the surface z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2 (only where z >= 0)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=cyan!25, draw=cyan!55!black,
          fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.35,
          domain=-2:2, y domain=-1.41:1.41, samples=15, samples y=13]
  {4 - x^2 - 2*y^2};
% the cutting plane y = 1
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, domain=-2:2, y domain=0:3.6,
          fill=cyan!45, fill opacity=0.22, draw=cyan!50!black,
          draw opacity=0.35]
  ({x},{1},{y});
% the trace C1: z = 2 - x^2 in the plane y = 1
\addplot3[very thick, blue!55!black, domain=-1.41:1.41, samples=33]
  ({x},{1},{2 - x^2});
% tangent line at (1,1,1), slope -2 in the x-direction
\addplot3[very thick, magenta, domain=-0.85:0.85, samples=2]
  ({1+x},{1},{1 - 2*x});
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8, color=magenta]
  coordinates {(1,1,1)};
\node[magenta, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.15,1,0.75) {$(1,1,1)$};
\node[blue!55!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.0,0.66) {$C_1$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:1.0,0.66)
  -- (axis cs:-1.15,1,0.677);
\node[cyan!50!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.0,0.46) {$y=1$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:1.0,0.46)
  -- (axis cs:-1.7,1,0.5);
\node[cyan!55!black, anchor=south] at (axis description cs:0.30,1.0)
  {$z=4-x^2-2y^2$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.30,1.0)
  -- (axis cs:-0.6,-0.7,2.66);
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Similarly, <m>f_y(x,y) = -4y</m>, so
<md>
f_y(1,1) = -4.
</md>
The intersection of the surface and the plane <m>x = 1</m> is the
parabola <m>z = 3 - 2y^2</m>, and <m>f_y(1,1) = -4</m> is the slope of
the tangent line to that curve, located in the <m>x = 1</m> plane. This
is the curve <m>C_2</m> in <xref ref="fig-pd-elliptic-y"/>. The tangent
line is steeper than the one in <xref ref="fig-pd-elliptic-x"/>, which
matches <m>|-4| \gt |-2|</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-pd-elliptic-y">
<caption>
The trace <m>C_2</m> cut from <m>z = 4 - x^2 - 2y^2</m> by the plane
<m>x = 1</m> is the parabola <m>z = 3 - 2y^2</m>. Its tangent line at
<m>(1,1,1)</m> has slope <m>f_y(1,1) = -4</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
The same dome shaped surface, now cut by the vertical plane x equals one.
The curve of intersection is drawn in dark blue and labelled C two, and a
magenta tangent line touches it at the point 1, 1, 1. This tangent line
is noticeably steeper than the one in the previous figure.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=10cm, height=9cm,
  view={-40}{16},
  xmin=-2.1, xmax=2.1, ymin=-1.6, ymax=1.6, zmin=0, zmax=4.4,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  ticks=none, clip=false,
]
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=cyan!25, draw=cyan!55!black,
          fill opacity=0.42, draw opacity=0.35,
          domain=-2:2, y domain=-1.41:1.41, samples=15, samples y=13]
  {4 - x^2 - 2*y^2};
% the cutting plane x = 1
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, samples=2, domain=-1.41:1.41, y domain=0:3.6,
          fill=cyan!45, fill opacity=0.22, draw=cyan!50!black,
          draw opacity=0.35]
  ({1},{x},{y});
% the trace C2: z = 3 - 2y^2 in the plane x = 1
\addplot3[very thick, blue!55!black, domain=-1.22:1.22, samples=33]
  ({1},{x},{3 - 2*x^2});
% tangent line at (1,1,1), slope -4 in the y-direction
\addplot3[very thick, magenta, domain=-0.55:0.55, samples=2]
  ({1},{1+x},{1 - 4*x});
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8, color=magenta]
  coordinates {(1,1,1)};
\node[magenta, anchor=west] at (axis cs:1.1,1.25,0.85) {$(1,1,1)$};
\node[blue!55!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.0,0.70) {$C_2$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:1.0,0.70)
  -- (axis cs:1,-0.85,1.555);
\node[cyan!50!black, anchor=west] at (axis description cs:1.0,0.50) {$x=1$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:1.0,0.50)
  -- (axis cs:1,-1.3,0.6);
\node[cyan!55!black, anchor=south] at (axis description cs:0.30,1.0)
  {$z=4-x^2-2y^2$};
\draw[gray!60, thin] (axis description cs:0.30,1.0)
  -- (axis cs:-0.6,-0.7,2.66);
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-pd-second-derivatives">
<title>Second Derivatives and the Mixed Derivative Theorem</title>

<p>
We also define second derivatives such as
<md>
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x \partial y},
</md>
by which we mean that we first differentiate with respect to <m>y</m> and
then with respect to <m>x</m>. We can alternatively denote such a second
derivative by <m>f_{yx}</m>. Likewise
<md>
\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2}
</md>
means that we differentiate with respect to <m>x</m> twice, and we can
alternatively denote such a second derivative by <m>f_{xx}</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-pd-second">
<title>Computing Second Partial Derivatives</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy^2)</m> and evaluate the
following partial derivatives.
<ol marker="A.">
<li><p><m>f_{yx}</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_{xy}</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>f_{xx}</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
<alert>A.</alert>
<md>
f_{yx} = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}
\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\cos(xy^2)\right)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(-y^2\sin(xy^2)\right)
= -2y\sin(xy^2) - 2xy^3\cos(xy^2)
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>B.</alert>
<md>
f_{xy} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\cos(xy^2)\right)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(-2xy\sin(xy^2)\right)
= -2y\sin(xy^2) - 2xy^3\cos(xy^2)
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>C.</alert>
<md>
f_{xx} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\cos(xy^2)\right)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(-y^2\sin(xy^2)\right)
= -y^4\cos(xy^2)
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<theorem xml:id="thm-mixed-derivative">
<title>The Mixed Derivative Theorem (Clairaut's Theorem)</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>f(x,y)</m> and its partial derivatives <m>f_x</m>, <m>f_y</m>,
<m>f_{xy}</m>, and <m>f_{yx}</m> are defined throughout an open region
containing a point <m>(a,b)</m> and are all continuous at <m>(a,b)</m>,
then
<md xml:id="eq-mixed-derivative" number="yes">
f_{xy}(a,b) = f_{yx}(a,b).
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Consider again <m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy^2)</m> from
<xref ref="example-pd-second"/>. We computed <m>f_{xy}</m> and
<m>f_{yx}</m>, and if you compare the two results, you can see that the
order of differentiation does not matter, which clearly follows from the
mixed derivative theorem.
</p>

<p>
The practical value of <xref ref="thm-mixed-derivative"/> is that we may
choose whichever order makes the computation easier, as the next example
shows.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-pd-mixed-shortcut">
<title>Choosing the Convenient Order</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider
<m>f(x,y) = \cos(x^2 y) + \dfrac{e^{(y^2+y+1)}}{y^2+5}</m> and calculate
<m>f_{xy}</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
We notice that the mixed derivative theorem applies, so
<m>f_{xy} = f_{yx}</m>, and that it is faster to compute
<m>f_{yx}</m>. The reason is that differentiating with respect to
<m>x</m> first annihilates the entire second term, which depends on
<m>y</m> alone and is unpleasant to differentiate:
<md>
f_{xy} = f_{yx} = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(
\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\cos(x^2 y)
+ \frac{e^{(y^2+y+1)}}{y^2+5}\right)\right)
= \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(-2xy\sin(x^2y)\right)
= -2x\sin(x^2y) - 2x^3y\cos(x^2y).
</md>
Hence
<md>
f_{xy} = f_{yx} = -2x\sin(x^2y) - 2x^3y\cos(x^2y).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
